course: dictionary
lesson: 8863
title: Shoplifting
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Shoplifting is built from two parts: shop and lifting. To lift something is to pick it up, so shoplifting means picking things up in a shop and leaving without paying. The word itself gives you the clue.
explain: Context helps too. «Taking something from a shop without paying is called shoplifting, and it is stealing, even if it is only one 80p chocolate bar.» The sentence tells you shoplifting is stealing, and that even a small thing counts. Try your meaning in the sentence: "stealing from a shop without paying" fits.
explain: Shoplifting is a crime, so you will meet it in safety and citizenship lessons. «Consequences of shoplifting can include being banned from the shop, a call to parents, and the police.» Being caught can cause real trouble, and a warning can stay on your record.
fact: Shoplifting means taking things from a shop without paying; it is stealing.
fact: Shoplifting is a crime, even for small things like sweets or one grape from the fruit pile.
fact: «Caught shoplifting» means a person was found stealing from a shop.
fact: Shops use cameras, tags and guards to stop shoplifting.
fact: Shoplifting can raise prices for everyone, because shops lose money on the goods that are stolen.
fact: Shoplifting is the noun, as in «shoplifting is a crime». Shoplift is the verb, as in «she shoplifted a pen».
exercise: «Taking things from a shop without paying is called what?» | *Shoplifting | Browsing | Paying in advance | Window shopping
exercise: «Shoplifting is a crime, even for one sweet.» Why does the sentence say even one sweet? | To show that sweets are bad for your teeth | To show that sweets are cheap | To show that sweets are free in shops | *To show that small things can still count as shoplifting
exercise: Which word is the verb in «She shoplifted a pen»? | she | a | *shoplifted | pen
test: What do shops use to stop shoplifting? | Longer opening hours | Free sweets for everyone | *Cameras, tags and guards | Bigger shopping bags
test: Why can shoplifting make prices go up? | Shops get more customers when things are stolen | *Shops lose money on stolen goods, so they charge more to make up for it | Prices go down when things are stolen | Shoplifters pay extra at the till
exam: Which is the best meaning of shoplifting? | Going into a shop to look around | *Taking things from a shop without paying | Working behind the counter in a shop | Buying things in a shop with a card
